# Tests

Tests run on **Vitest through vite-plus**. Unit tests live next to what they cover
(`src/core/index.test.ts`); the end-to-end suite is the `test/` workspace (below). `pnpm test`
runs both — build first, `test/` runs `dist` — and one unit file is
`pnpm -F wakusei test src/<path>.test.ts`.

## Shape

- Import from `vite-plus/test`: `import { describe, expect, it, vi } from 'vite-plus/test'`.
- `it`, never `test` (`vitest/consistent-test-it`). A title describes the behavior in a sentence.
- A table is **`it.each` / `describe.each`**, never a loop around `it` (`vitest/prefer-each`).
- **Exact matches only**: `toBe` / `toStrictEqual`. `toContain`, `toMatch` and other partial
  matchers are banned — they miss formatting drift and import-order changes. The one exception is
  array membership (`expect(files).toContain('posts.ts')`). Generated code is compared as the
  whole string, or as the exact list of lines that matter.
- An equality matcher over `toBe(true)` on a comparison (`vitest/prefer-equality-matcher`);
  `toThrow` names its message (`vitest/require-to-throw-message`). A mock names its type
  parameters (`vi.fn<(id: string) => void>()`).
- Hooks sit at the top of their `describe`, in order
  (`prefer-hooks-on-top`, `prefer-hooks-in-order`). `xit` / `fit` prefixes and identical titles
  fail lint; never commit `it.only` or `it.skip`.

## What tests may do that `src` may not

`*.test.ts` may use `any`, `as` / `as never` casts, `let`, empty stub callbacks and cross-layer
imports when they spell the fixture out most clearly — coverage wins over strictness at the test
boundary. The `vite.config.ts` override for `**/*.test.ts` lists exactly what is relaxed;
everything else in the TypeScript rule still applies. Test files sit outside `tsconfig.json`, so
type-aware lint sees the default lib there: sort a fresh array with `.sort()`, not `toSorted`.

## Working with the filesystem

- Generators resolve paths against `process.cwd()`. A test that writes files creates its own
  directory (`fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'wakusei-…'))`), `process.chdir`s into it,
  and restores the previous cwd and removes the directory in `afterEach`. That is why the suite runs
  with `pool: 'forks'`.
- Run a generator Effect with `runGenerator` / `runGeneratorError` from `src/testing`; decode a
  config for it with `parseConfig`, the way the CLI does.
- Parsing TypeSpec / OpenAPI and formatting real output is slow under a parallel run;
  `testTimeout` is 30s for that reason. Do not lower it to find a hang.
- The Vite plugin suite (`vite-plugin/index.test.ts`) drives the real generator through a mock
  dev server, so it proves what lands on disk (a handler body surviving a regeneration).
  `vite-plugin/concurrency.test.ts` mocks `../core/index.js` with a deliberately slow first run,
  because only a run held open can show whether a second one starts beside it. Background work is
  awaited with `eventually` (`vi.waitFor` with a 20s timeout — its 1s default is too short for a
  real generation under a parallel run) on its observable result, never a fixed sleep in the test,
  and must finish before the test ends or its writes and log lines land in the next test.
- The CLI suite runs `wakusei(argv, entryUrl)` with a recording `Console`, so it asserts exactly
  what a user sees on stdout and stderr. `cli/watch.test.ts` forks the command with `--watch`,
  edits real files and interrupts the fiber the way Ctrl-C does. An edit can land before the OS
  watcher is registered, so `writeUntil` repeats it until the output shows the pass; the budgets
  are starvation allowances, not expectations.

## The end-to-end suite (`test/`)

- `scripts/generate.mjs` runs the packaged CLI (`packages/wakusei/dist/cli.js`) in every
  `cases/<name>/` that has a `wakusei.config.ts`, writing to `__generated__/<name>` (gitignored).
  A case with an `overlay` link gets `overlays/handlers` copied in first — handler bodies a user
  already wrote — so the run exercises the merge, and the merged files are what gets checked.
- `scripts/typecheck.mjs` runs `tsc -p cases/<name>` for every case with a `tsconfig.json`,
  under strict, `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` and `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, against the real
  `@orpc/*`, zod, valibot and arktype. A generated stub next to an `.output()` does not
  type-check until implemented, which is what the overlay is for.
- `runtime/*.test.ts` read `__generated__`: the routers answer HTTP through oRPC's
  `OpenAPIHandler` (coercion, validation, success status, merged bodies), `typecheck.test.ts` runs
  the script above, and `vite-plugin.test.ts` starts a real Vite dev server with `wakuseiVite()`.
- A new generator feature gets a case (a config, and a tsconfig to type-check it) and, when it
  changes behavior over HTTP, an assertion in `runtime/`. Overlays name procedures, so a spec
  change that renames one updates `overlays/handlers` in the same change.

## Coverage of behavior

- A bugfix ships with a test that fails before it and passes after. Prove it: revert the fix,
  watch the test fail, restore it.
- Assert through the observable result — files on disk and their contents, the generated source,
  log lines — rather than internals. A test is not a reason to export a function.
- A change to the generated code is checked across all three schema libraries and both modes.
